FIND(1)                        P32W Unix                         FIND(1)

NAME
       find - walk directory trees, testing each path

SYNOPSIS
       find [path ...] [expression]

DESCRIPTION
       find walks each path (default: the current directory, .)
       and every file and directory below it, each directory before
       its contents, in directory order (not sorted), and evaluates
       the expression for each. Paths are built from the path as
       given, so find . gives ./bin/sh, find /usr gives /usr/ken
       and find d/ gives d/x. The paths are the arguments before
       the first one that starts with -, or is ! or (.

       With no expression every path is printed. An expression
       with no -print or -exec in it is printed where it is true,
       as if it were ( expression ) -print.

PRIMARIES
       -name pattern
              true if the last component matches pattern: * any
              string, ? any one character, [...] one of the
              characters (a-z ranges, ! or ^ first to negate).
              Quote it from the shell.
       -type c
              true for a plain file (f), directory (d), character
              device (c), block device (b) or pipe (p).
       -newer file
              true if modified more recently than file.
       -print print the path; true.
       -exec command [arg ...] ;
              run command with the arguments, each {} replaced by
              the path; true if it exits with status 0. The ; must
              be quoted for the shell: \;.

OPERATORS
       ! expr        true if expr is false
       expr -a expr  both (also just expr expr); the second is
                     not evaluated if the first is false
       expr -o expr  either; the second is not evaluated if the
                     first is true
       ( expr )      grouping; quote the parentheses: \( \)

       ! binds tightest, then -a, then -o.

EXAMPLES
       find /usr/ken                     everything under ken's home
       find . -name '*.c'                C sources here and below
       find / -type d                    every directory
       find . -name '*.o' -exec rm {} \; remove object files
       find src -newer stamp -print      changed since stamp
       find . \( -name '*.c' -o -name '*.h' \) -type f

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every path was examined; 1 if one could not be (a path
       that does not exist, a directory that cannot be read, a path
       longer than 255 characters: each is reported); 2 for an
       error in the expression (an unknown primary, a missing
       argument or ;, a misplaced operator or parenthesis), when
       nothing is walked. The status of -exec commands does not
       count.

SEE ALSO
       ls(1), du(1), grep(1), sh(1)

BUGS
       There are no -user, -perm, -mtime, -size, -prune, -depth or
       -ok primaries, and {} is replaced only as a whole word.
